1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,000
DocPipe turns the scans in a corporate credit file into filed, checked and
searchable documents.

2
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,000
A credit officer drops three scans: a loan contract, a supplier invoice photo,
and a restaurant menu that does not belong.

3
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,061
Each file becomes a job. Page images are read by a vision model; pages with a
text layer are read for free.

4
00:00:03,061 --> 00:00:10,064
The live log shows every step: OCR, classification against a fixed taxonomy, the
checks, filing and publishing.

5
00:00:10,064 --> 00:00:15,467
While the jobs run, the System tab. Every service is healthy, and the monitor
reads the audit log.

6
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:21,781
It tracks review rate, queue age, schema errors, latency and cost per document,
against thresholds in the config.

7
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:28,839
A real alert is firing: one review ticket has waited longer than the 24-hour
limit. Alerts also go to a team webhook.

8
00:00:28,839 --> 00:00:44,266
Back in the inbox, the jobs are finishing. (waiting shown 10x faster)

9
00:00:44,266 --> 00:00:54,646
The contract and the invoice were filed automatically. The menu is outside the
taxonomy, so it waits for a person.

10
00:00:54,646 --> 00:01:00,967
The contract is filed under the borrower, Hung Thinh, not under the bank printed
first: company, year, document type.

11
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:08,552
The page image sits next to the OCR text, so anyone can check a line against the
original.

12
00:01:08,552 --> 00:01:19,415
A contract has no tables, so two deterministic checks gate auto-filing: the
quoted evidence and the company name must really appear in the text.

13
00:01:19,415 --> 00:01:29,817
The invoice goes to the buyer, the bank's customer, not to the seller printed at
the top. Six test documents are built to trap exactly this.

14
00:01:29,817 --> 00:01:40,197
The review queue: the menu waits with its reasons. Nothing in this queue reaches
the knowledge base until a named reviewer approves it.

15
00:01:40,197 --> 00:01:46,681
Staff ask questions in Vietnamese. OpenViking ranks the documents; our own index
picks the page and enforces the caller's clearance.

16
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:55,613
The answer cites the page it came from. A question the documents cannot answer
is refused instead of guessed.

17
00:01:55,613 --> 00:02:02,005
One click on the citation opens the exact page.

18
00:02:02,005 --> 00:02:13,520
Inside OpenViking, published documents mirror the same folder tree, with masked
text and a summary per document.

19
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:21,461
Every time a filed document is replaced, the earlier version is kept.

20
00:02:21,461 --> 00:02:32,206
Rolling back restores it to its folder, re-publishes it, and writes the
reviewer's name to the audit log. A whole bad release can be undone the same
way.

21
00:02:32,206 --> 00:02:38,332
If something looks wrong, the kill switch sends every new document to review.
One click, logged with who did it.

22
00:02:38,332 --> 00:02:44,332
A model reads; our checks decide; a person approves what is uncertain. All data
shown is synthetic or public.
